<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Healing Plants of Panama</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Soothing Guanabana</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Clarify misunderstandings in group discussions. Communication: Interpret cultural differences in communication.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can clarify misunderstandings in group discussions by pointing to visual aids (e.g., points to a picture of sabila (aloe vera) and says, "This is for burns."). Can interpret cultural differences in communication by explaining remedies and using culturally relevant expressions or gestures (e.g., says, "This is used to heal." while pointing to a leaf and using the Panamanian expression dale to indicate agreement or encouragement). Can explain simple plant remedies for peers, using visuals (e.g., points to a leaf and says, "This helps skin.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing flashcards of different healing plants (soursop, aloe vera, lemongrass, mint leaves, ginger). Students quickly call out the names. The teacher then asks, "What are these plants *used for*?" to activate prior knowledge. Students share short answers in pairs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to clarify misunderstandings about remedies. Teacher holds up a picture of *sábila* (aloe vera) and says, "This is *sábila*." Teacher then pretends to misunderstand, "Is *sábila* for headaches?" Teacher then clarifies, "No, *sábila* is *used to* heal burns and skin infections. You *should* apply the gel directly. It's a natural *treatment* for your skin." The teacher stresses "heal" and "treatment" while gesturing. Teacher also models the pronunciation of compound nouns like "herbal garden," stressing the first syllable.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is *sábila* good for stomachaches?" (Expected answer: No.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What *should* you use *sábila* for?" (Expected answer: For burns/skin infections.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Healing Plants of Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short informational text (e.g., a short notice from a local health clinic) about the *guanábana* (soursop) plant, detailing its parts, uses, and traditional application methods. The text includes expressions like "traditionally, it is used to..." and advice like "you *shouldn't* consume too much raw fruit." It also features an explanation of what an *herb* is and its *therapeutic application*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret cultural differences in communication related to health remedies, stating the purpose of plants using the infinitive of purpose, and giving advice using 'should/shouldn't'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the text individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (True/False):</b> "True or False: *Guanábana* leaves are *used to* treat infections." "True or False: You *should* eat a lot of raw *guanábana* fruit every day."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match parts of the *guanábana* plant (leaf, fruit) to their specific *remedy* or *treatment* mentioned in the text.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using the infinitive of purpose (*used to* + infinitive verb) and 'should/shouldn't' for giving advice, along with the target vocabulary (plant, leaf, root, remedy, ginger, infections, treatment, herb, application, soursop, aloe vera, lemongrass, mint leaves, treat, heal, soothe, apply, grow, boil, prepare, useful, medicinal, natural, therapeutic, for, with, traditionally, safely). Focus on phonemic awareness by practicing syllable division in words like "treatment" and "therapeutic."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Cloze Activity:</b> Students complete sentences about various plants (e.g., *ginger*, *lemongrass*) using "is used to..." or "are used to..." and specific verbs. For example: "Ginger ______ (soothe) colds." (Expected: is used to soothe). "Mint leaves ______ (relieve) headaches." (Expected: are used to relieve).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Advice Chain:</b> Students work in small groups. The teacher provides a common ailment or situation (e.g., "a small cut," "feeling stressed"). One student states the problem, and the next student gives advice using "You *should*..." or "You *shouldn't*..." and mentions a plant. (E.g., "I have a small cut." -> "You *should* use *sábila* for that. It helps to *heal*." -> "My head hurts." -> "You *should* drink *hierba de limón* tea. It's *used to* soothe."). The teacher monitors for correct use of grammar and pronunciation, especially for "therapeutic."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Explain and Clarify:</b> Students work in pairs. Student A chooses a healing plant from a list (e.g., *guanábana*, *aloe vera*, *mint leaves*) and briefly explains its *remedy* using a visual. Student B listens and then asks a clarifying question or summarizes what they understood, aiming to show they understood the purpose or advice. Student A must then clarify using simpler language or by pointing to the visual. (e.g., Student A: "This is *guanábana* leaf. It is *used to* treat infections." Student B: "So, it's for when I have a fever?" Student A: "No, not really for fever. It's for infections, like when your skin gets infected or for internal *infections*. It helps to *heal* those." While pointing to a picture of an infected area.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Healing Plants of Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Peer Explanation and Clarification of Healing Plant Guide (21st Century Skills Project).</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each student brings their "Healing Plant Guide" (developed in previous lessons) which includes descriptions of 2-3 plants, their uses (infinitive of purpose), and advice (should/shouldn't). One student presents a chosen plant from their guide to their group members, explaining its *remedy* and *application* using visual aids from their guide. The other group members act as peers who might need clarification or want to understand cultural nuances.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student takes turns presenting one plant from their "Healing Plant Guide."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The presenter explains: "This is [plant name]. It is *used to* [purpose]." and "You *should* [advice for application]." (e.g., "This is *lemongrass*. It is *used to* relieve colds. You *should* boil the leaves and drink it as a tea.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Group members listen and are encouraged to ask clarifying questions: "So, is it only for colds?" "Can I just eat the leaf?" "What does 'soothe' mean?" or "What's the best *treatment*?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The presenter must then clarify any misunderstandings, perhaps using different words, gestures, or pointing to their visual aid. They can also incorporate culturally relevant expressions (e.g., "Yes, *dale*, it helps a lot!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students rotate roles until everyone has presented and clarified for at least one plant.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Task:</b> The teacher observes students during the group activity, specifically noting their ability to explain remedies, clarify misunderstandings, and use the target linguistic structures (infinitive of purpose, should/shouldn't) and vocabulary. A quick observation checklist can be used.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation when presenting a plant remedy.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective clarification of misunderstandings using appropriate language and visuals.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of "is/are *used to*" + infinitive.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of "should/shouldn't" for advice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of culturally relevant expressions or gestures (e.g., "dale").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially of two- and three-syllable words and compound nouns.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students write their answers to the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One plant remedy I explained well today was... because I used...</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One thing I learned about clarifying information or interpreting differences in communication was...</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How confident do I feel explaining healing plant remedies to others and clarifying when they don't understand?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was the most challenging part of explaining or clarifying today, and how can I improve it?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The mediation activities worked well because students actively engaged in clarifying misunderstandings and using visual aids from their project. The integration of the "Healing Plant Guide" as the core production task for mediation was highly effective in encouraging authentic communication and application of the target language.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Some adjustments needed: Provide more sentence starters for asking clarifying questions for lower-proficiency students (e.g., "Do you mean...?", "Can you explain that again?"). Explicitly prompt students to use the Panamanian expression "dale" more often to encourage cultural interpretation. Some students struggled with the pronunciation of "therapeutic" – more targeted practice on syllable division is needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Healing Plants of Panama" theme. The next lesson will transition to a new theme, likely starting with Lesson 1 (Listening & Vocab Intro) of a new unit, building on students' ability to understand and communicate about practical topics.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>